      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level effect: the preliminary-print mean supplies the central anchor. Momentum effect: firm private demand and recent consumption growth favor output growth exceeding modest labor-input growth. One-off effect: inventory, trade and annual source-data revisions have no assigned directional adjustment. Policy-mechanism effect: this statistical release has no directly announced policy setting, so no separate policy adjustment is applied."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The Q2 employment-hours archive fetch failed, so a Q3-versus-Q2 aggregate-hours growth rate could not be calculated from fetched evidence. Monthly private hours are only a directional indicator: BLS productivity hours also include proprietors and other workers. The forecast therefore uses small judgmental adjustments rather than claiming a complete output-per-hour nowcast."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: Use an analyst-chosen mean-reversion/persistence prior, 0.75 times the eight-print mean plus 0.25 times the latest preliminary print: 0.75*1.8625 + 0.25*1.4 = 1.746875. Add judgmental adjustments of +0.2 percentage point for demand momentum and +0.1 for subdued hours momentum; one-off and policy adjustments are zero. This gives 2.046875%, rounded to 2.0%. For this change series, compute dispersion from the fetched print values themselves: sum((x-1.8625)^2) = 19.37875; sigma = 1.6638488084 = sqrt(19.37875/7). A normal-approximation 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 2.1297264747 percentage points. Bounds around the rounded point are -0.1297264747% and 4.1297264747%, rounded to -0.1% and 4.1%. No additional widening or narrowing is applied; small-sample calibration remains a limitation."
